raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How did partisan polarization in online discussions of lockdowns, masks, and vaccines vary across U.S. states, Canadian provinces, and events in late 2020?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our work addresses this need by providing a comprehensive, large-scale study of geographic and event-driven variation in online partisan polarization surrounding COVID-19 discussions across American states and Canadian provinces.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we examine regional variations in discussions surrounding three key\nhealth interventions: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is online polarization associated with how conservative or liberal a region is?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "tweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions;",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is polarization in vaccine discussions associated with subsequent regional vaccination rates?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Next, we explore the relationship between vaccine-related polarization and actual vaccination rates\nacross US states.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "This relationship was notably\nweaker in Canada, pointing to important cross-national differences in how political\npolarization manifests and impacts health behaviors.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the amount of discussion about COVID-19 conspiracy theories move together with daily aggregate polarization?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, we explored the relationship between conspiracy discourse and polarization using time-resolved measurements\nof conspiracy-related tweets compared with aggregate daily polarization.",
            "page": 20
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors challenge the assumption that linguistic differences alone indicate polarization: can comparing partisan groups on the same contentious topic yield a better proxy?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "However, when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Comparing how partisans talk about the same public health intervention shows that pandemic polarization was neither uniform nor stable: it varied by place, political leaning, topic, and event, and took different forms in the two countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "By analyzing millions of tweets and\ntracking semantic differences between partisan groups across different regions and topics, we have demonstrated that\npolarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "By tracking the temporal evolution of polarization, we identify distinct spikes linked to specific political events and\npolicy announcements.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-national comparison suggests that local political and institutional conditions may shape whether online partisan divisions translate into differences in health behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This suggests that the impact of online polarization on public health behaviors may be mediated by broader political and institutional factors.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The proposed text-based framework is intended to measure polarization at fine spatial, temporal, and topical scales beyond the pandemic case.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our study focuses on the COVID-19 context, the proposed\nmethodology for quantifying partisan polarization at scale can be adapted to examine large-scale discussions across\nvarious temporal, spatial, and topical dimensions.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors collected a 1% real-time Twitter sample via the official API from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021. Political and pandemic filtering yielded about 12.55 million U.S. tweets and 46.64 million Canadian tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Lockdowns, masks, and vaccines were selected as salient examples of interventions involving collective behavior, individual behavior, and medicine, respectively.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These topics were\nselected for their salience in polarized discourse [27,43,44], and because they represent different types of interventions\n(group behavior, individual behavior, and medicine, respectively).",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Topic labeling started with keywords and hashtags. Two political scientists annotated the frequent hashtags, and the authors supplemented the resulting list with hashtags from prior research.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For each topic,\ntweets were classified as relevant or irrelevant based on whether they contained at least one topic-specific keyword.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.\n3. We merged these with hashtags identified in previous studies for the same topics [45–47].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors adapted RoBERTa separately to each country's corpus and fine-tuned the topic classifiers using manually reviewed tweets and lower-weight hashtag labels.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We then domain-adapted RoBERTa-base models [41] on the COVID-19 tweets from each country’s dataset through\nself-supervised learning (predicting masked words within tweets), creating country-specific pre-trained language\nmodels.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists independently reviewed each tweet to determine relevance, discarding\ntweets where consensus couldn’t be reached. We then fine-tuned the respective domain-adapted RoBERTa models to\nclassify by topics.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "During training, we also incorporated hashtag-annotated tweets as weak supervision, assigning them a reduced loss weight of 0.9 compared to 1.0 for\ngold-standard labels.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The conspiracy measure counts discussion, not endorsement: tweets promoting a theory and tweets debunking it both qualify. A tweet could be assigned to more than one topic.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors geolocated users from profile text when Open Street Map and ArcGIS agreed within one degree. The regional distribution of geolocated users was checked against census counts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and\nlongitude coordinates when a location is found. We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other, finding this approach more accurate than using pre-trained Named Entity Recognition\nalgorithms.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Correlation is done with the official 2021 population census for each country.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Party inference used a profile stage and an activity stage: explicit profile signals supplied labels, and a multilayer perceptron inferred affiliation from users' averaged tweet embeddings.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We then used these profile-based classifications as labels to train a classifier based on user activity\npatterns.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the activity classifier, we generated user embeddings by aggregating tweet embeddings\nfrom each user (using mean pooling), then trained a multi-layer perceptron to predict party affiliation.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The U.S. analysis compared Democrats with Republicans. The Canadian analysis grouped Liberals, New Democrats, and Greens on the left and Conservatives and People's Party supporters on the right.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional ratios of inferred liberal to conservative users were validated against official election results, with correlations of 0.802 in the United States and 0.815 in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the U.S. and Canada, we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada (visualized in Fig 3 and Fig 2).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A separate U.S. validation matched users to voter-registration records in five states and Washington, DC. The profile and activity classifiers agreed with the records in about three quarters of cases.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For further external validation of our U.S. party affiliation\npredictions, we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization index averages relevant tweet embeddings into one vector per user and uses the C-index to score how distinctly party groups cluster. The score ranges from zero to one.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This index ranges from 0 (no polarization) to 1 (extreme polarization where partisan groups are completely isolated from\neach other in the embedding space).",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To make the index feasible on large datasets, the authors repeatedly subsampled users until the estimates stabilized. In tests on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets, absolute error fell below 0.001 at about 10,000 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold (e.g., 10 [48]).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "We validated our approximation method on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets. As shown in Fig 4, the absolute error\ndrops dramatically at around 3,000 users and falls below 0.001 at approximately 10,000 users.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The geographic analysis averaged weekly polarization over 12 weeks and compared regional rankings with partisan voting in the 2020 U.S. presidential election and the 2019 Canadian federal election.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Color intensity from light to dark gives the amount of polarization measured weekly between October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 and then averaged\nover the 12 weeks.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "When examined against partisan voting patterns (using the 2020 U.S. presidential election and 2019 Canadian federal\nelection), we observe a clear trend: conservative states and provinces show higher levels of polarization compared\nto their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External health data included official case and death reports and regional vaccination coverage for at least two doses, measured a year after the Twitter collection period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.\nFor the United States, we sourced data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). For Canada, we use\nthe data reported by the Canadian government.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection. The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The temporal analysis tracked national polarization daily, examined both preselected events and events inferred from the largest peaks, and averaged polarization around the identified events.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In particular, we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization (Tables 9 and 10, shown as red circles in Fig 13).",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "To evaluate the overall responsiveness of our measure to polarizing events, we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.",
            "page": 19
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservative regions generally had higher polarization, but the evidence was stronger in the United States: Republican electoral strength was significantly associated with polarization over masks and vaccines, not lockdowns. Canada had lower overall polarization and no statistically significant regional vote-margin associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The geographic pattern had important exceptions. Liberal Delaware ranked first for lockdown polarization, while conservative Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas were among the least polarized on that topic. Quebec had Canada's highest vaccine polarization.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The lockdown discourse shows a more mixed pattern, with outliers from both liberal\nand conservative states; namely Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas showing the least polarization, and Delaware (ranked 1),\nColorado (ranked 17), and New Jersey (ranked 15) showing higher values.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "Our results confirmed that Quebec had the highest\nlevel of partisan division over vaccines, alongside the highest reported COVID-19 incidence in Canada during the first\nand second pandemic waves [89].",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. regions with more polarized vaccine discussion had substantially lower later vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not show the same negative pattern; the supplementary estimate excluding Nunavut was positive (r = 0.74) and based on 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The correlation is −0.77 with CI = [−0.86, −0.62] (n = 51, p = 6.97e-11).",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarization rose and fell sharply over a few days. Many peaks coincided with salient events, including the U.S. presidential election in lockdown discourse and the Moderna rollout and approval in Canadian vaccine discourse.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For lockdown-related discourse (Fig 13A), significant polarization peaks occurred around the 2020 Presidential Election.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "Regarding vaccines (Fig 13F), the two highest peaks occurred on December 20 and 23, 2020, coinciding with the\nUS distribution of the Moderna COVID-19 vaccines and Health Canada’s approval of the Moderna vaccine [56], respectively.",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.",
            "page": 19
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives posted about conspiracy theories at higher rates in both countries, but daily conspiracy-tweet volume was weakly negatively associated with aggregate polarization in the United States and was not significantly associated with it in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "show that conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.",
            "page": 21
          },
          {
            "text": "In the United States (B), we find there is a statistically significant correlation −0.247 with\nCI=[−0.448,-0.023] (n = 88, p = 0.031) between these measures. In Canada (D), we find that there is no statistically significant correlation of 0.023 with\nCI=[−0.187,0.231] (n = 88, p = 0.831) between these measures.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The index measures semantic similarity as a proxy, not every form of partisan polarization; it can miss signals that the embeddings do not represent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Averaging tweets at the user level can obscure individual rhetorical moves such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because our measure aggregates tweets into user-level embeddings, it captures\noverall partisan alignment at the user level, rather than fine-grained nuances in individual tweets. As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Canadian results do not distinguish parties within each ideological family and omit Bloc Québécois and other minor-party supporters.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Second, in the Canadian context specifically, we categorized users into liberal (left) and conservative (right) party families. During manual annotation of Twitter (X) profiles, we encountered few users who identified\nas Bloc Québécois supporters and excluded them from analysis.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Inferred affiliation is not definitive: self-reports can be inaccurate, voter registration can be outdated or diverge from current online leanings, and name matching can miss users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "especially considering that voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online. We note that name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names;",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Twitter discourse is not representative of either country's general public; it captures a more politically engaged and often more partisan subset.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, our analysis is based on Twitter (X) data, which may not fully capture the views and sentiments of the broader\nAmerican and Canadian public. Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The English-only sample primarily captures anglophones and bilingual francophones in Canada, which may bias estimates for Quebec in particular.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fourth, we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias. For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Geographic assignments can be wrong because locations are self-reported and may be incomplete, imprecise, or false.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "that self-reported Twitter locations may be incomplete, imprecise, or even intentionally false\n(including the rare influence of VPNs);",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Automated accounts may exaggerate apparent partisan separation by posting or amplifying disproportionate amounts of slanted content.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically\nslanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political\ngroups.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Different collection keywords may account for some apparent cross-national differences: U.S. collection emphasized elections, while Canadian collection included province names and explicit COVID terms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should\nbe considered when interpreting the comparative results.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The late-2020 Twitter data and later vaccination data are separated in time. Their association is suggestive, not proof that polarization caused changes in uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Seventh, there are temporal mismatches between our Twitter dataset (collected in late 2020) and regional vaccination rates, which occurred primarily in 2021. As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors draw no firm conclusion from the Canadian vaccination correlation because it is based on few regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The timing of peaks does not establish that particular events caused them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analysis does not identify a single explanation for why conservative regions were more polarized.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization, state-specific trajectories provide insights.",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not quantify the precise impact of social networks on polarization or health outcomes, or determine how cross-border information flows shaped polarization.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation, especially as online communities increasingly\ntranscend national boundaries while still being influenced by local political contexts.",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "As our analysis demonstrates,\nsocial networks clearly contributed to the diffusion of partisan opinions during the COVID-19 pandemic, but quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend computational research that uses contextual text embeddings to study polarization. They treat embeddings as complementary to dictionary methods and choose a text-only design rather than relying on increasingly restricted social-network data.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Embedding-based models\ncapture latent semantic distinctions between ideological groups, providing methodological advantages that complement\ntraditional lexicon- or dictionary-based strategies.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "However, access to user- and\nnetwork-level data has become increasingly restricted, particularly under recent platform policy changes. Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends earlier research on COVID-19 partisanship rather than rejecting it: it finds similar divisions and adds a finer-grained account of where they appeared, how they changed, and which events coincided with them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution. However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Don Albrecht's cited study reports that vaccination rates are lower where Republican vote share is higher. The authors build on that result by showing that a separate measure, polarization in online vaccine discussions, also correlates negatively with later U.S. vaccination rates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Importantly, we observe that vaccine polarization is strongly negatively correlated with actual vaccination rates\nacross American states (Fig 12). This aligns with the well-documented negative correlation between Republican vote\nshare and vaccination rates [50].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Albrecht D. Vaccination, politics and COVID-19 impacts.",
            "page": 29
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
